@meta
id: 13-13
title: Very magic
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-verymagic
par: 30
minutes: 6
vscode: Ctrl+F with the regex toggle on — this is the dialect it uses
open: versions.yaml

@brief
Every service in this file is pinned to a full `major.minor.patch`
version. Two of them are not — they stop at `major.minor` — and the
deploy script rejects them. Append `.0` to each of the two-part
versions. The `tooling` block is out of scope and is not pinned the
same way.

You know how to write this pattern now: a colon, a space, some digits,
a dot, some digits, and then the end of the line.

    /: \d\+\.\d\+$

Four backslashes in ten characters, three of them on quantifiers. You
have been typing patterns like this for three lessons. There is a
switch that makes vim's dialect look like everyone else's, and this
lesson is where you turn it on.

@keys
| `\v` | very magic — from here on, every punctuation character is special |
| `/\v: \d+\.\d+$` | the same pattern, one backslash instead of four |
| `+` `?` `{` `(` `\|` `<` `>` | bare, under `\v`, they all mean what they mean elsewhere |
| `\.` `\(` `\+` | under `\v`, a backslash makes a special character literal |
| `A` then `n.` | append, next, repeat |

@hints
Write the pattern you already know, then take the backslashes off the
quantifiers and put two characters at the front.
--
`\v` at the start of a pattern switches to very magic. Under it, `+`
is one-or-more without a backslash, and so are `?`, `{}`, `()` and
`|`. The dot still needs `\.` to be literal.
--
`/\v: \d+\.\d+$` `Enter`, then `A.0`, `Esc`, and `n.` for the second.

@solution
/\v: \d+\.\d+$<Enter>  A.0<Esc>  n.  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-five keystrokes, and the pattern reads the way it would in
JavaScript, in `grep -E`, in the VS Code find box with regex on.

Vim has four magic levels, set by a prefix at the start of a pattern.
Two matter:

    magic        the default   `\+ \? \{ \( \| \<`   escaped to be special
    very magic   `\v`          `+ ? { ( | <`         special on their own

The other two, `\M` and `\V`, go the other way — `\V` "very nomagic"
makes *everything* literal except the backslash, which is the right
tool when you want to search for a path or a chunk of code full of
dots and brackets and do not want to escape any of it.

Under `\v` the rule is simple: every ASCII character except letters,
digits and underscore is special. That is the same rule as most regex
engines, give or take, so a pattern you would write anywhere else can be
pasted in after `\v` and will usually work. The cost is the reverse
one: a literal `(`, `+`, `|`, `=`, `@` or `<` now needs its backslash.
`\v\(` is a literal bracket. `\v\=` is a literal equals sign — and that
one bites, because `=` is a quantifier under very magic, the same as
`?`.

Should you use it? Most people who write a lot of patterns do, and
start every non-trivial search with `\v` by reflex. The escaped
dialect is still worth knowing because you will read it constantly in
other people's config and in every answer online. Know both; type
`\v`. From here on, this chapter does.

@start versions.yaml
services:
  order-api: 2.4.1
  order-worker: 2.4.1
  gateway: 1.14.2
  ledger: 0.9.12
  notifier: 1.2
  reconciler: 3.0.0
  scheduler: 1.7.4
  mysql: 8.4
  rabbitmq: 3.13.7

tooling:
  node: 22
  pnpm: 9
  prisma: 5.14.0

@expected versions.yaml
services:
  order-api: 2.4.1
  order-worker: 2.4.1
  gateway: 1.14.2
  ledger: 0.9.12
